// and an additional enhancer that applies a custom middleware named “myMiddleWare”.
const store = manageReactState({
  // Create the Redux store (or equivalent) using your reducers and initial state,
  // applying the logger middleware during creation.
  store: createStore(
    reducersMap,
    initialStateObject,
    applyMiddleware(loggerMiddleware)
  ),

  // Provide a React context that will expose the store to the component tree.
  context: MyAppContext,

  // Expose custom hooks for selecting state and dispatching actions.
  hooks: {
    useStateSelectorHook,
    useDispatchActionHook
  },

  // Add any enhancers – here we add an enhancer that applies a custom middleware
  // called “myMiddleWare” (you can replace this with your actual enhancer logic).
  enhancers: [applyMiddleware('myMiddleWare')]
});
```

> **Explanation**  
> * `store`: The actual Redux (or similar) store instance created with your reducers, initial state, and the logger middleware.  
> * `context`: A React context object (`MyAppContext`) that will provide the store to the component tree.  
> * `hooks`: An object containing your custom hooks (`useStateSelectorHook` and `useDispatchActionHook`) so components can consume and update state.  
> * `enhancers`: An array of enhancers – here we pass an enhancer that applies a custom middleware named `"myMiddleWare"`.  

> **Note**  
> Replace `createStore`, `applyMiddleware`, `MyAppContext`, `useStateSelectorHook`, and `useDispatchActionHook` with the actual implementations from your project.

